    <parser-implementation-note>
      Initial parser used Enum.TryParse + Enum.IsDefined guards. First test run exposed two Enum.TryParse behaviours that broke my own parser tests: (a) it accepts numeric strings ("0"→Model1Shared) and (b) it silently trims whitespace before matching. Neither is acceptable at the D-12 edge (H12c embeds the string byte-for-byte + Cosmos stores tenancyModel as a string; a numeric-string accept would produce distinct H12c keys for the same customer/model). Rewrote the parser body to scan Enum.GetNames + string.Equals(Ordinal) directly — no numeric acceptance, no auto-trim. Documented in the parser's own file header for the next reader.
    </parser-implementation-note>

    <bff-parallel-enum-note>
      Preflight grep surfaced an existing name-compatible `TenancyModel` enum at `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Registration/DataverseEnvironmentRecord.cs` (task 023 v3 addition, `Sprk.Bff.Api.Services.Registration` namespace). Identical shape (Model1Shared=0, Model2Dedicated=1). The two enums live in separate assemblies with no cross-reference; both serve internal handler branching in their own layer. BFF's enum is used by BFF's Registration service layer; ControlPlane's enum (new) is used by L2 handlers. Any Dataverse row's tenancyModel string round-trips identically whichever service reads it. Future consolidation into a shared Contracts assembly is legitimate refactoring but is OUT OF SCOPE for Task 223 (no §6.5 conflict here — the parallel enum was pre-existing and is not touched).
    </bff-parallel-enum-note>

